    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarians typically ground their stance in classical liberalism and natural rights theory, arguing that taxing individuals infringes on their property and that the government should only exist to protect against force or fraud. In contrast, social democrats rely on communitarian and social contract theories, asserting that societal well-being and systemic inequality justify progressive taxation as a moral imperative to ensure basic liberties for all. Both sides present a \"strong\" case by framing liberty and responsibility as essential preconditions for a just society, yet they fundamentally disagree on where the state should intervene to balance these competing values.",
